{{Nihongo|'''Nagashima'''|長島|Nagashima|"Long Island"}} is a major riverine island located in the central estuarine delta of [[Okami]], the capital metropolis of [[Okaiken]]. Stretching approximately 6 kilometres in length but averaging only 200 metres in width, the highly developed landmass serves as the primary civic center and the most concentrated district for high-rise skyscraper development in the city. 

==Geography==
Nagashima is geographically defined by its extreme length and narrow profile. At its widest point near the northern end, the island spans roughly 700 metres across, but it severely tapers as it extends southward, maintaining an average width of only 200 metres for the majority of its 6-kilometre length. 

The island is completely encased by the Garyū River to the west and the Sōryū River to the east. These two waterways, historically referred to as the "Inner Claw" rivers, separate Nagashima from the merchant hub of Mugishima and the entertainment districts of Tamashima. Because of its narrow width, virtually every major building on the island enjoys waterfront views, and the island is connected to the rest of the metropolis by a series of densely packed pedestrian and vehicular bridges.

==History==
Like the other four central islands of Okami, Nagashima is an artificial landmass created during the early 17th century. Following the relocation of the Honda clan to the region, Honda Shigetomo initiated the "Dragon's Claw Division," a massive terraforming project designed to tame the flood-prone delta of the Kiso and Kashi rivers. Millions of tons of excavated river silt were piled onto the marshland and secured by interlocking granite retaining walls, raising Nagashima above the tidal flood lines.

During the Honda Shogunate, Nagashima was designated as the civic and administrative core just south of the heavily fortified Tenkyō-jima. Because of its proximity to Okami Castle, the long island was partitioned into sprawling, highly manicured estates for the Shogun's most trusted retainers and elite samurai class. These historic estates featured defensive walls, expansive courtyards, and private docks along the Garyū and Sōryū rivers for immediate access to the castle moat.

During the Great Adonian War in the mid-20th century, the historic wooden estates of Nagashima were completely eradicated by incendiary bombing, leaving the island a flattened expanse of ash. 

==Modern development==
During the post-war economic miracle and the subsequent urban population explosion of the 1960s, the Okami Metropolitan Government completely reimagined the island's purpose. In 1968, new municipal zoning laws were established to protect the historical character of the old castle town on Tenkyō-jima by restricting building heights. To accommodate the booming corporate sector, Nagashima was explicitly rezoned for large-scale, hyper-dense skyscraper development.

Today, Nagashima is synonymous with Okami's modern skyline. The island's extreme geographic constraints have forced architects to build vertically, resulting in a continuous, 6-kilometre corridor of towering glass and steel commercial high-rises, luxury hotels, and corporate headquarters.

